\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Streamlit visualisation of McNemar contingency tables and statistics for two monitored models. The top panel corresponds to \texttt {gpt-5}, where no discordant pairs occur, and the bottom panel to \texttt {gpt-5.1-mini}, where the discordant counts $(b,c)=(13,7)$ yield a non-significant drift signal.}}{12}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Overview of the deployment utility dashboard in the monitoring application. The top panel recalls the definition of $U_t(m)$ and exposes controls for the evaluation window, model set, and the cost-sensitivity parameter $\lambda $.}}{14}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Per-model metrics and utility values for $\lambda = 10$ on the 300-example monitoring subset. The highlighted row shows the model selected by maximising $U_t(m)$, jointly accounting for accuracy and cost.}}{15}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Utility $U_t(m)$ as a function of the cost-sensitivity parameter $\lambda $ for all candidate models. The gradual flattening of the curves with increasing $\lambda $ reflects the diminishing marginal penalty introduced by the $\log (1 + C_t(m))$ term.}}{15}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Same utility-vs-$\lambda $ plot as in Figure~\ref {fig:utility-vs-lambda}, but with a single model highlighted to make the effect of increasing $\lambda $ on its deployment attractiveness more visible.}}{16}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Generic iterative loop of automatic prompt optimisation. Different APO methods instantiate different candidate-generation, evaluation, and selection operators.}}{19}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Taxonomy of automatic prompt optimisation methods. Illustration adapted from~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ramnath2025survey}.}}{19}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Role of prompt optimisation inside the monitoring pipeline. APO is triggered only after a statistically supported degradation event.}}{28}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Overall system architecture: dataset and trace layers in Langfuse, batch execution, monitoring, and adaptation via model switching or GEPA-based prompt optimisation.}}{30}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Artifact flow from the Langfuse dataset to dashboard and analysis outputs.}}{31}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Automated weekly monitoring loop implemented via GitHub Actions.}}{32}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Decision logic after a monitoring run. Drift is tested on the fixed 300-example subset. If no statistically significant degradation is detected, the current setup is retained. If degradation is detected, corrective actions are compared under the same deployment utility \(U(m)\); this may lead either to model switching or to prompt optimisation with subsequent re-evaluation.}}{36}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Prompt adaptation flow after a detected degradation signal. Recovery is measured on the same fixed monitoring subset using paired counts and utility changes.}}{37}{figure.caption.22}%
